  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a two-level optimization strategy: a coarse search over several grid sizes (n_points) with stochastic initialization, followed by a fine-tuning phase using the best solution from the coarse search. We start from the given initial solution (45 points) and then successively refine it by increasing the grid resolution (90, 180, etc.) while enforcing constraints through projected gradient optimization. This approach differs from previous strategies by using a multi-resolution hierarchy and incorporating analytical insights to improve convergence.\n\nThe algorithm's strengths include:\n- Progressive grid refinement balances global exploration and local exploitation\n- Constraint handling preserves valid solutions throughout the optimization\n- Transfer mechanism extends successful patterns to higher resolutions\n- Parallel evaluation accelerates the search\n\nMajor limitations:\n- High computational cost due to full correlation computations\n- Requires careful handling of value constraints\n- Limited by available gradient estimation methods\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nVariables:\n- n_points (number of samples): integer starting at 45, doubled successively to 90, 180...\n- h(x): vector of length n_points, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n- dx = 2.0/n_points (step size)\n- Smoothness parameter \u03c4 (determines interpolation rate for transfer)\n- Trade-off factors \u03bb, \u03b1, \u03b2\n\nObjective: Minimize\n    C_5 = (np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * dx\nwhere dx = 2.0/n_points\n\nConstraints:\n- Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- Bounded box: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n\nImplementation:\n- Use finite difference Jacobian due to unavailable autograd\n- Enforce bounds within objective evaluation\n- Preserve global minimum shift invariant property trough cross-entropy transfer\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nParent Initialization:\nInitialize n_levels = [45] + [90*i for i in (2,4,8,...) until time limit\nh0 = initial_h_values (45-point solution)\n\nLoop over grid levels:\n    A. Coarse Sample: Generate N_init=10 random samples per grid level (S) satisfying:\n          sum(h) = n_points/2 exactly\n          0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n\n    B. Running Optimization:\n        - Sample initialization: Latin Hypercube sampling over feasible region\n        - For each sample, minimize using SLSQP with finite-difference Jacobian\n        - Track best solution (minimal C_5)\n\n    C. Progressive Enhancement:\n        - After finding candidate solution (h_l), transfer it to higher grid via:\n            S = S * adptive transfer_matrix (nearest-neighbor preserving)\n        - Augment with new search domain to maintain diversity\n\n    D. Constraint Enforcement:\n        - Apply projection operator to enforce 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n        - Adjust integral externally via multiplicative scaling\n\nTransition to higher grid:\n    h_next = interpolate_weights(h, \u03c4) * scaling_factor\n    normalizing = np.sqrt(np.sum(transfer_matrix(diag=\u03c4))\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\nGrid Sizes: [45, 90, 180, 360] (initial and adaptive doubling until time/budget)\nInitial Samples: 10 per grid level (LSH with rejection sampling for feasible solutions)\nPopulation Dynamics: Trigger grid expansion when current level stagnates (10^4 evaluations)\n diversity_maintenance = 0.8   # retain 80% of population\n\nConstraint: Integral = sum(h) = n_points/2 (for dx=2/n_points)\nProjection: h_proj = np.clip(h,0,1); h_proj *= (45/np.sum(h_proj))   # force integral\nWarmStart: From best transfer solution at each grid level (but keep global best)\n\nTransfer:\n    Parameters: \u03bb=0.9, \u03c4=0.8 (conservation parameter)\n    Method: Histogram matching with local descent for shift diversity\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\nCritical Path:\n- Correlation (O(n^2)): 45^2=2025 op, 90^2=8100, scaling factor ~4\n- Budget: 1100s maximum evaluation time (wall-clock)\n\nDeployment Strategy:\n- Run 2 concurrent grid levels (45 and 90 points)\n- Increase grid sizes adaptively (monitor wall-clock)\n- Graceful degradation: Discard worst grids when time constraints\n\nFallback Mechanism:\n- Preserve solution after grid failure\n- Recurse if current grid exceeds time limit\n- Hyperparameter search: Adapt \u03c4, \u03c1 based on lack of improvement\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\nFeasibility Checks:\n1. 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n2. abs(sum(h)-n_points/2) \u2264 1e-5\n3. Objective returns float in [0, previous record]\n\nOutput Structure:\nreturn (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nIncludes:\n- New best solution to date\n- Current grid requirements\n- Value checks before return\n\nImplementation includes safety valves:\n- Early termination if solution overshoots budget\n- Value clipping enforcement in core modules\n- Progress recording to enable resumption\n- Multi-function evaluation to catch invalid states",
